The v1.14 pointer-to-member-function triviality exemption landed on
the AArch64 and MSVC overlays but missed the Itanium x86-64 copy of
the rule, so extern "C++" functions RETURNING a member fn pointer
by value got a hidden sret buffer the C++ side (which returns PMFs
in RAX:RDX per the SysV classification) never wrote. First executed
against a real C++ compiler by the g++/libstdc++ CI leg running
examples/member_fn_ptr: SIGSEGV dispatching through the garbage
pointer.
---
 compiler/rustc_target/src/callconv/x86_64.rs | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/compiler/rustc_target/src/callconv/x86_64.rs b/compiler/rustc_target/src/callconv/x86_64.rs
index 428c6add8df..7bc5c4c5873 100644
--- a/compiler/rustc_target/src/callconv/x86_64.rs
+++ b/compiler/rustc_target/src/callconv/x86_64.rs
@@ -382,9 +382,18 @@ pub(crate) fn compute_cxx_abi_info<'a, Ty, C>(cx: &C, fn_abi: &mut FnAbi<'a, Ty>
     // forwarders generate). Without this rule, a `#[repr(C)]`
     // 8-byte struct would come back in `rax` while C++ writes to
     // `rdi` → garbage. Symmetric to the AArch64 overlay.
+    //
+    // v1.15.0: pointer-to-member-function pairs are exempt here
+    // too — g++/clang return them in RAX:RDX. The v1.14 exemption
+    // landed on the AArch64 + MSVC overlays but missed this
+    // Itanium x86-64 copy; nothing executed it against a real C++
+    // compiler until the g++/libstdc++ Linux CI leg ran
+    // examples/member_fn_ptr (SIGSEGV: Rust passed a hidden sret
+    // buffer that g++ never wrote).
     if !fn_abi.ret.is_ignore()
         && (fn_abi.ret.layout.is_cxx_non_trivial_for_calls(cx)
             || fn_abi.ret.layout.is_adt())
+        && !fn_abi.ret.layout.is_cxx_member_fn_ptr(cx)
     {
         fn_abi.ret.force_indirect();
     }
